INSERT INTO `material_chunks` VALUES (1,1,1,1,1,0,NULL,NULL,'Why your body needs both systems','Every cell in your body releases energy from food through cellular respiration. This process uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide as a waste product. The respiratory system brings oxygen into the body and removes carbon dioxide. The circulatory system carries both gases between the lungs and the rest of the body. Neither system can do the job alone.',354,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(2,1,1,1,1,1,NULL,NULL,'The path air takes','When you breathe in, air enters through the nose or mouth. Hairs and mucus in the nasal cavity trap dust, and the air is warmed and moistened. The air passes the pharynx (throat) and the larynx (voice box), then travels down the trachea (windpipe). Rings of cartilage keep the trachea open.\n\nThe trachea divides into two bronchi, one for each lung. Inside the lungs the bronchi branch into smaller and smaller tubes called bronchioles, which end in clusters of tiny air sacs called alveoli.',490,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(3,1,1,1,1,2,NULL,NULL,'Gas exchange in the alveoli','The lungs contain hundreds of millions of alveoli. Their walls are only one cell thick and each one is wrapped in a net of capillaries. Oxygen diffuses from the air in the alveoli, where its concentration is high, into the blood, where its concentration is lower. Carbon dioxide diffuses the other way, from the blood into the alveoli, and is breathed out.\n\nThree features make this exchange fast: the very large total surface area of the alveoli, their thin walls, and their moist lining, which lets gases dissolve before they diffuse.',536,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(4,1,1,1,1,3,NULL,NULL,'How breathing works','Breathing is driven mainly by the diaphragm, a dome-shaped muscle under the lungs. When you inhale, the diaphragm contracts and flattens, and the muscles between the ribs pull the rib cage up and out. The chest cavity gets bigger, the air pressure inside the lungs drops below the pressure outside, and air flows in.\n\nWhen you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves back up, the rib cage moves down and in, the chest cavity gets smaller, and air is pushed out.',461,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(5,1,1,1,1,4,NULL,NULL,'The heart and its four chambers','The heart is a muscular pump about the size of your fist. It has four chambers: two upper atria and two lower ventricles. The right side of the heart receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs. The left side receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it to the rest of the body.\n\nValves between the chambers and at the exits of the ventricles keep blood flowing in one direction. The left ventricle has the thickest muscular wall because it must pump blood all the way around the body.',518,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(6,1,1,1,1,5,NULL,NULL,'Blood vessels','Arteries carry blood away from the heart. Their walls are thick and elastic so they can handle high pressure. Veins carry blood back to the heart. They have thinner walls and contain valves that stop blood from flowing backward. Capillaries are the smallest vessels. Their walls are one cell thick, which lets oxygen, nutrients, carbon dioxide and other wastes pass between the blood and the body\'s cells.',405,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(7,1,1,1,1,6,NULL,NULL,'Two circuits working together','Blood travels in two circuits. In pulmonary circulation, blood goes from the right ventricle to the lungs, picks up oxygen, and returns to the left atrium. In systemic circulation, blood goes from the left ventricle to the tissues of the body, delivers oxygen, and returns to the right atrium.\n\nWhen you exercise, your muscles need more oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide. You breathe faster and more deeply, and your heart beats faster and pumps more blood with each beat. This is how the two systems respond together to the body\'s needs.',543,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(8,1,1,1,1,7,NULL,NULL,'Keeping both systems healthy','Smoking damages the lining of the airways and the walls of the alveoli, and it makes the heart work harder. Regular physical activity strengthens the heart muscle and helps the lungs work efficiently. Avoiding second-hand smoke and wearing a well-fitted mask during heavy smoke or haze protect the airways. A balanced diet that limits excess salt and saturated fat helps keep blood vessels healthy.',398,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(9,1,1,1,1,8,NULL,NULL,'Check your understanding','1. Trace the path of an oxygen molecule from the air you breathe to a muscle cell in your leg.\n2. Explain why the walls of the alveoli and the capillaries are so thin.\n3. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker wall than the right ventricle?',244,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(10,1,1,2,2,0,1,1,NULL,'Heredity: inheritance and variation\nGenes, chromosomes and DNA\nTraits are characteristics that are passed from parents to their offspring, such as the shape of a seed or the colour of a flower. The instructions for traits are carried by genes.\nA gene is a segment of DNA, and genes are found on chromosomes inside the nucleus of a cell.\nHuman body cells contain 46 chromosomes arranged in 23 pairs. One chromosome of each pair comes from the mother and the other comes from the father. Because chromosomes come in pairs, most genes come in pairs too.\nDifferent versions of the same gene are called alleles. For example, a gene for stem length in pea plants has one allele for tall stems and another allele for short stems.',722,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(11,1,1,2,2,1,2,2,NULL,'Mendel\'s pea plant experiments\nGregor Mendel, a monk who worked in the 1800s, studied inheritance by crossing pea plants that showed contrasting traits, such as tall and short stems.\nWhen Mendel crossed pure-breeding tall plants with pure-breeding short plants, all of the offspring in the first generation, called F1, were tall. When he let the F1 plants self-pollinate, about three-fourths of the second generation, called F2, were tall and about one-fourth were short.\nFrom these results Mendel concluded that each plant carries two factors for a trait, which we now call alleles. One allele can hide the effect of the other: the allele that shows is dominant and the hidden one is recessive. The two alleles separate when sex cells (gametes) are formed, so each gamete carries only one allele. This idea is called the law of segregation.',841,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(12,1,1,2,2,2,3,3,NULL,'Using a Punnett square\nGeneticists write the dominant allele with a capital letter and the recessive allele with the same letter in lowercase. For stem length, T stands for tall and t stands for short.\nThe combination of alleles an organism has is its genotype. The trait that shows is its phenotype. An organism with two identical alleles, TT or tt, is homozygous. An organism with two different alleles, Tt, is heterozygous. A heterozygous pea plant is tall, because T is dominant.\nA Punnett square shows all the possible allele combinations from a cross. In a cross between two heterozygous plants (Tt x Tt), the offspring genotypes are TT, Tt, Tt and tt. The genotypic ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, and the phenotypic ratio is 3 tall : 1 short. The probability that any one offspring is short is 1 in 4, or 25 percent.',821,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(13,1,1,2,2,3,4,4,NULL,'Patterns beyond simple dominance\nNot every trait follows simple dominance. In incomplete dominance, the heterozygous offspring shows a phenotype in between the two parents. When a red-flowered four o\'clock plant is crossed with a white-flowered one, the offspring have pink flowers.\nIn codominance, both alleles are fully expressed. Roan cattle have both red hairs and white hairs. In human ABO blood types, the A and B alleles are codominant, so a person who inherits both has type AB blood.\nSome genes have more than two alleles in a population. The ABO blood type gene has three alleles: IA, IB and i. The i allele is recessive, so a person with type O blood has the genotype ii.\nSex-linked traits are controlled by genes on the X chromosome. Red-green colour blindness is more common in males because males have only one X chromosome, so a single recessive allele on that X chromosome is enough for the trait to appear.',923,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(14,1,1,3,3,0,NULL,NULL,'Vocabulary to know','- Gene, allele, chromosome\n- Dominant and recessive\n- Genotype and phenotype\n- Homozygous and heterozygous\n- Incomplete dominance and codominance',145,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(15,1,1,3,3,1,NULL,NULL,'Practice problems','1. In guinea pigs, black fur (B) is dominant over white fur (b). What fraction of the offspring of a Bb x bb cross is expected to have white fur?\n2. Two pink four o\'clock flowers are crossed. List the expected phenotypes of the offspring and their ratio.\n3. A mother has type A blood with genotype IAi and a father has type B blood with genotype IBi. Which blood types are possible for their children?',401,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(16,1,1,4,4,0,NULL,NULL,'Before the activity','- Read the whole procedure before you start.\n- Tie back long hair and keep bags under the table.',96,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(17,1,1,4,4,1,NULL,NULL,'During the activity','- Stop exercising and sit down if you feel dizzy or short of breath.\n- Tell your teacher right away about any spill or injury.',126,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(18,1,2,5,5,0,NULL,NULL,'Why your body needs both systems','Every cell in your body releases energy from food through cellular respiration. This process uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide as a waste product. The respiratory system brings oxygen into the body and removes carbon dioxide. The circulatory system carries both gases between the lungs and the rest of the body. Neither system can do the job alone.',354,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(19,1,2,5,5,1,NULL,NULL,'The path air takes','When you breathe in, air enters through the nose or mouth. Hairs and mucus in the nasal cavity trap dust, and the air is warmed and moistened. The air passes the pharynx (throat) and the larynx (voice box), then travels down the trachea (windpipe). Rings of cartilage keep the trachea open.\n\nThe trachea divides into two bronchi, one for each lung. Inside the lungs the bronchi branch into smaller and smaller tubes called bronchioles, which end in clusters of tiny air sacs called alveoli.',490,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(20,1,2,5,5,2,NULL,NULL,'Gas exchange in the alveoli','The lungs contain hundreds of millions of alveoli. Their walls are only one cell thick and each one is wrapped in a net of capillaries. Oxygen diffuses from the air in the alveoli, where its concentration is high, into the blood, where its concentration is lower. Carbon dioxide diffuses the other way, from the blood into the alveoli, and is breathed out.\n\nThree features make this exchange fast: the very large total surface area of the alveoli, their thin walls, and their moist lining, which lets gases dissolve before they diffuse.',536,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(21,1,2,5,5,3,NULL,NULL,'How breathing works','Breathing is driven mainly by the diaphragm, a dome-shaped muscle under the lungs. When you inhale, the diaphragm contracts and flattens, and the muscles between the ribs pull the rib cage up and out. The chest cavity gets bigger, the air pressure inside the lungs drops below the pressure outside, and air flows in.\n\nWhen you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves back up, the rib cage moves down and in, the chest cavity gets smaller, and air is pushed out.',461,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(22,1,2,5,5,4,NULL,NULL,'The heart and its four chambers','The heart is a muscular pump about the size of your fist. It has four chambers: two upper atria and two lower ventricles. The right side of the heart receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs. The left side receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it to the rest of the body.\n\nValves between the chambers and at the exits of the ventricles keep blood flowing in one direction. The left ventricle has the thickest muscular wall because it must pump blood all the way around the body.',518,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(23,1,2,5,5,5,NULL,NULL,'Blood vessels','Arteries carry blood away from the heart. Their walls are thick and elastic so they can handle high pressure. Veins carry blood back to the heart. They have thinner walls and contain valves that stop blood from flowing backward. Capillaries are the smallest vessels. Their walls are one cell thick, which lets oxygen, nutrients, carbon dioxide and other wastes pass between the blood and the body\'s cells.',405,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(24,1,2,5,5,6,NULL,NULL,'Two circuits working together','Blood travels in two circuits. In pulmonary circulation, blood goes from the right ventricle to the lungs, picks up oxygen, and returns to the left atrium. In systemic circulation, blood goes from the left ventricle to the tissues of the body, delivers oxygen, and returns to the right atrium.\n\nWhen you exercise, your muscles need more oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide. You breathe faster and more deeply, and your heart beats faster and pumps more blood with each beat. This is how the two systems respond together to the body\'s needs.',543,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(25,1,2,5,5,7,NULL,NULL,'Keeping both systems healthy','Smoking damages the lining of the airways and the walls of the alveoli, and it makes the heart work harder. Regular physical activity strengthens the heart muscle and helps the lungs work efficiently. Avoiding second-hand smoke and wearing a well-fitted mask during heavy smoke or haze protect the airways. A balanced diet that limits excess salt and saturated fat helps keep blood vessels healthy.',398,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(26,1,2,5,5,8,NULL,NULL,'Check your understanding','1. Trace the path of an oxygen molecule from the air you breathe to a muscle cell in your leg.\n2. Explain why the walls of the alveoli and the capillaries are so thin.\n3. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker wall than the right ventricle?',244,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(27,1,3,6,6,0,NULL,NULL,'What makes an equation quadratic','A quadratic equation can be written in standard form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, where $a$, $b$ and $c$ are real numbers and $a \\neq 0$. The highest power of the variable is 2. A quadratic equation has at most two real solutions, also called roots.',241,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(28,1,3,6,6,1,NULL,NULL,'Method 1: Extracting square roots','Use this method when there is no $x$ term, that is, when $b = 0$. Isolate $x^2$, then take the square root of both sides. Remember that a positive number has two square roots.\n\nExample: $x^2 - 49 = 0$ gives $x^2 = 49$, so $x = 7$ or $x = -7$.',242,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(29,1,3,6,6,2,NULL,NULL,'Method 2: Factoring','Write the equation in standard form, factor the left side, then use the zero product property: if $pq = 0$, then $p = 0$ or $q = 0$.\n\nExample: $x^2 + 5x + 6 = 0$ factors as $(x + 2)(x + 3) = 0$, so $x = -2$ or $x = -3$.\n\nAlways check by substituting. For $x = -2$: $(-2)^2 + 5(-2) + 6 = 4 - 10 + 6 = 0$.',303,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(30,1,3,6,6,3,NULL,NULL,'Method 3: Completing the square','Rewrite the equation so that one side is a perfect square trinomial.\n\nExample: $x^2 + 6x - 7 = 0$. Move the constant term: $x^2 + 6x = 7$. Add the square of half the coefficient of $x$, which is $3^2 = 9$, to both sides: $x^2 + 6x + 9 = 16$, so $(x + 3)^2 = 16$. Then $x + 3 = 4$ or $x + 3 = -4$, which gives $x = 1$ or $x = -7$.',329,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(31,1,3,6,6,4,NULL,NULL,'Method 4: The quadratic formula','The quadratic formula works for every quadratic equation in standard form:\n\n$$x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\nExample: $2x^2 - 3x - 2 = 0$ has $a = 2$, $b = -3$ and $c = -2$. Then $b^2 - 4ac = 9 + 16 = 25$, so $x = \\frac{3 \\pm 5}{4}$, which gives $x = 2$ or $x = -\\frac{1}{2}$.',287,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(32,1,3,6,6,5,NULL,NULL,'The discriminant','The expression $b^2 - 4ac$ is called the discriminant. It tells you the nature of the roots before you solve:\n\n- If $b^2 - 4ac > 0$, there are two distinct real roots.\n- If $b^2 - 4ac = 0$, there is exactly one real root, called a repeated root.\n- If $b^2 - 4ac < 0$, there are no real roots.',292,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(33,1,3,6,6,6,NULL,NULL,'Choosing a method','Extracting square roots is quickest when $b = 0$. Factoring is efficient when the trinomial factors easily over the integers. Completing the square shows where the quadratic formula comes from and helps you write a quadratic function in vertex form. The quadratic formula always works, so use it when the other methods are awkward or when the roots are not rational.',366,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(34,1,4,7,7,0,NULL,NULL,'What makes an equation quadratic','A quadratic equation can be written in standard form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, where $a$, $b$ and $c$ are real numbers and $a \\neq 0$. The highest power of the variable is 2. A quadratic equation has at most two real solutions, also called roots.',241,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(35,1,4,7,7,1,NULL,NULL,'Method 1: Extracting square roots','Use this method when there is no $x$ term, that is, when $b = 0$. Isolate $x^2$, then take the square root of both sides. Remember that a positive number has two square roots.\n\nExample: $x^2 - 49 = 0$ gives $x^2 = 49$, so $x = 7$ or $x = -7$.',242,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(36,1,4,7,7,2,NULL,NULL,'Method 2: Factoring','Write the equation in standard form, factor the left side, then use the zero product property: if $pq = 0$, then $p = 0$ or $q = 0$.\n\nExample: $x^2 + 5x + 6 = 0$ factors as $(x + 2)(x + 3) = 0$, so $x = -2$ or $x = -3$.\n\nAlways check by substituting. For $x = -2$: $(-2)^2 + 5(-2) + 6 = 4 - 10 + 6 = 0$.',303,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(37,1,4,7,7,3,NULL,NULL,'Method 3: Completing the square','Rewrite the equation so that one side is a perfect square trinomial.\n\nExample: $x^2 + 6x - 7 = 0$. Move the constant term: $x^2 + 6x = 7$. Add the square of half the coefficient of $x$, which is $3^2 = 9$, to both sides: $x^2 + 6x + 9 = 16$, so $(x + 3)^2 = 16$. Then $x + 3 = 4$ or $x + 3 = -4$, which gives $x = 1$ or $x = -7$.',329,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(38,1,4,7,7,4,NULL,NULL,'Method 4: The quadratic formula','The quadratic formula works for every quadratic equation in standard form:\n\n$$x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\nExample: $2x^2 - 3x - 2 = 0$ has $a = 2$, $b = -3$ and $c = -2$. Then $b^2 - 4ac = 9 + 16 = 25$, so $x = \\frac{3 \\pm 5}{4}$, which gives $x = 2$ or $x = -\\frac{1}{2}$.',287,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(39,1,4,7,7,5,NULL,NULL,'The discriminant','The expression $b^2 - 4ac$ is called the discriminant. It tells you the nature of the roots before you solve:\n\n- If $b^2 - 4ac > 0$, there are two distinct real roots.\n- If $b^2 - 4ac = 0$, there is exactly one real root, called a repeated root.\n- If $b^2 - 4ac < 0$, there are no real roots.',292,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(40,1,4,7,7,6,NULL,NULL,'Choosing a method','Extracting square roots is quickest when $b = 0$. Factoring is efficient when the trinomial factors easily over the integers. Completing the square shows where the quadratic formula comes from and helps you write a quadratic function in vertex form. The quadratic formula always works, so use it when the other methods are awkward or when the roots are not rational.',366,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(41,1,5,8,8,0,NULL,NULL,'What an inference is','An inference is a logical conclusion you reach by combining clues in a text with what you already know. Writers do not state everything directly. They show actions, details and dialogue, and they expect readers to work out the rest.',232,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(42,1,5,8,8,1,NULL,NULL,'Clues plus knowledge','A useful formula is: text clues + what I know = inference. Text clues can be a character\'s actions, words or feelings, details of the setting, or the writer\'s choice of words. What you know comes from your own experience and from earlier reading.',246,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(43,1,5,8,8,2,NULL,NULL,'Steps for making an inference','1. Ask a question that the text does not answer directly, such as \"Why did the character leave early?\"\n2. Find two or three clues in the text that relate to the question.\n3. Connect the clues to what you already know.\n4. State your inference and point to the evidence that supports it.\n5. Check whether the rest of the text supports or challenges your inference.',362,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(44,1,5,8,8,3,NULL,NULL,'A short practice passage','Mara stood at the jeepney stop for the third time that week, clutching her umbrella even though the sky was clear. When the jeep finally came, she let two older women board first, then squeezed into the last seat near the door. She kept glancing at her wristwatch and tapping her foot.\n\nFrom the clues \"for the third time that week\", the repeated glances at her watch and the tapping foot, together with what we know about waiting for public transport, we can infer that Mara is worried about being late. Letting the older women board first suggests that she is considerate even when she is in a hurry.',602,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(45,1,5,8,8,4,NULL,NULL,'Common mistakes','- Making a guess that has no support in the text.\n- Ignoring clues that contradict your idea.\n- Confusing an inference with a summary of what the text says directly.',165,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(46,1,5,8,8,5,NULL,NULL,'Inference or prediction?','A prediction is a kind of inference about what will happen next. Like any inference, a prediction should be supported by clues from the text.',141,'2026-10-20 23:24:16'),(47,1,2,10,9,0,NULL,NULL,'A Quick Guide to Algebra','Learn the basics • Follow examples • Try it yourself\n\nWhat is algebra? Algebra uses letters (called variables) to represent numbers. An equation tells you that two expressions are equal.',186,'2026-10-22 19:59:07'),(48,1,2,10,9,1,NULL,NULL,'1. Know the key parts','Variable\n\nCoefficient\n\nEquation\n\nA letter for an unknown number, like x.\n\nThe number multiplied by a variable, like 4 in 4x.\n\nA statement that two sides are equal, like x + 3 = 8.',179,'2026-10-22 19:59:07'),(49,1,2,10,9,2,NULL,NULL,'2. How to solve equations','Example A: x + 3 = 8\n\nSubtract 3 from both sides.\n\nx + 3 − 3 = 8 − 3\n\nAnswer: x = 5\n\nExample B: 2x = 14\n\nDivide both sides by 2.\n\n2x ÷ 2 = 14 ÷ 2\n\nAnswer: x = 7',160,'2026-10-22 19:59:07'),(50,1,2,10,9,3,NULL,NULL,'3. Simplify like terms','Like terms have the same variable part. Add or subtract their coefficients: 3x + 2x = 5x. But 3x + 2 is not 5x because the terms are different.',143,'2026-10-22 19:59:07'),(51,1,2,10,9,4,NULL,NULL,'4. Practice','Solve for x: (1) x + 6 = 10 (2) 3x = 18 (3) 2x + 4 = 12 (4) 5x + 2x = ?\n\nAnswer key: (1) x = 4 • (2) x = 6 • (3) x = 4 • (4) 7x',127,'2026-10-22 19:59:07');
